REGIONAL REGULATION OF SOLOK CITY

NUMBER 2 OF 2020

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF SOLOK,

Considering:

a. that every person including women and children has the right to obtain protection from violence, exploitation, and discriminatory treatment in order to provide security and comfort;
b. that the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, discrimination, exploitation, and human rights violations in Solok City has not been optimal so that the role of Regional Government and society is required;
c. that to fill the legal vacuum related to the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, discrimination, exploitation and human rights violations, it is necessary to establish a regional regulation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Implementation of Protection for Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 8 of 1956 concerning Establishment of Small City Autonomous Regions Within the Central Sumatra Provincial Region (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1956 Number 19);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
6. Government Regulation Number 12 of 2017 concerning Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2017 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6041);
7. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 8 of 1970 concerning Implementation of the Government of Solok Municipality and Payakumbuh Municipality;
8. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 532);
9. Regional Regulation of West Sumatra Province Number 5 of 2013 concerning Protection of Women and Children (Regional Gazette of West Sumatra Province of 2013 Number 51 Supplement to the Regional Gazette of West Sumatra Province Number 88);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SOLOK CITY

and

MAYOR OF SOLOK

DECIDES:

To Stipulate:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. rights of women and children;
b. protection of women;
c. fulfillment of children's rightsd. special protection of children;
e. handling of women and children victims of violence;
f. establishment of UPTD PPA;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. community participation; and
i. financing.

Article 3

Every woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory actions;
b. obtain protection from economic and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain work leave while receiving salary or wages in accordance with statutory regulations;
e. obtain decent work according to her ability; 6f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and paths of education;
g. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with statutory regulations;
h. elect and be elected in general elections in accordance with statutory regulations;
i. obtain special protection in the implementation of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and or health related to women's reproductive function;  and
j. obtain health services in accordance with standards.

Article 4

Every child has the right to:
a. play, create, participate, grow and develop;
b. freely assemble, associate, live with parents, relate to parents if separated and survival;
c. obtain a name, identity, citizenship, education, child-appropriate information;
d. obtain health standards in accordance with decent living standards;
e. obtain protection and affection from parents;
f. obtain protection from exploitation as child labor, minority groups/isolated indigenous groups and from views or circumstances that by their nature are not yet appropriate for children to see;  and
g. obtain special protection in emergency/urgent situations, displacement/displaced persons/evicted persons, if experiencing legal conflicts and armed conflicts or social conflicts.

Article 6

The Regional Government in efforts to protect women is responsible for:
a. providing protection from discriminatory actions in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. providing protection from economic exploitation and social exploitation;
c. providing protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence and neglect;
d. providing equal opportunities with men to obtain employment in accordance with their abilities;
e. providing work leave while still receiving salary or wages in accordance with laws and regulations;
f. providing adequate health and maternity facilities for pregnant and nursing mothers;
g. providing comprehensive health services free of charge; 8h. guaranteeing the right of women to elect and be elected in general elections in accordance with laws and regulations;
i. providing special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and or health in relation to women's reproductive function;
j. providing special protection to women victims of violence and human trafficking;
k. taking preventive action in order to prevent violations against women; and
l. making regulations to regulate employers who employ women to comply with employment laws and regulations.

Article 7

The Regional Government in order to prevent violence against women is responsible for:
a. conducting socialization regarding prevention of violence against women to regional apparatus, community leaders, and community organizations;
b. optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations in preventing acts of violence;
c. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
d. involving community participation in the recovery and reintegration of women victims of violence;
e. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women; and
f. developing family resilience programs.

Article 12

1. The Regional Government in the context of fulfilling children's rights is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs, and activities for the fulfillment of children's rights;
b. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of fulfillment of children's rights;
c. conducting guidance and supervision in the context of implementing the fulfillment of children's rights;
d. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the child, birth order of the child, and physical and/or mental condition; and
e. cooperating with the private sector and/or non-governmental organizations in the implementation of fulfillment of children's rights.
2. The Regional Government in the context of fulfilling children's rights may cooperate with the community, business world, and mass media.
3. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to fulfill children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by regional apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 14

The community in efforts to fulfill children's rights is responsible for:
a. providing space to gather and organize as well as listening to children to express their opinions;
b. supervising children in accessing various information and providing healthy and safe information;
c. providing information through socialization and education regarding children's rights and laws and regulations concerning children; and
d. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of implementing the fulfillment of children's rights through child facilitators.

Article 18

The policy for protection and fulfillment of children's rights in the health field is as follows:
a. providing free health services for children from poor families;
b. organizing comprehensive health efforts for children;
c. ensuring that infants receive exclusive breast milk from their mothers;
d. ensuring that children receive nutrition that meets standards;
e. protecting children from efforts to transplant their body organs to other parties; and
f. ensuring the provision of monitoring of growth and development of toddlers and pre-school age children.

Article 20

Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 19 paragraph (2) letter a shall be carried out through socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyle patterns which include:
a. the importance of exclusive breast milk for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for infants and toddlers; and
c. the importance of a healthy lifestyle and balanced nutrition;

Article 21

Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 19 paragraph (2) letter b shall be implemented in order to prevent the occurrence of disease or health problems which are carried out by:
a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breast milk to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to infants and toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. providing monitoring of growth and development of toddlers and preschool-age children;
e. organizing comprehensive health efforts for children;
f. optimizing the role of integrated service posts in serving maternal and child health; and
g. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school.

Article 27

1. Every organizer of an education unit is prohibited from expelling children from educational institutions without guarantees for the continuity of the child's education.
2. Every organizer of formal education who violates the provisions as referred to in paragraph (1) may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
a. verbal warning;
b. written warning;
c. temporary suspension from activities;
d. termination of cooperation;
e. revocation of operational license; and
f. withdrawal of facilities.
3. Provisions regarding procedures for implementing administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 38

1. The Regional Government in the effort of special protection of children is responsible for:
a. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of special protection of children;
b. providing access to public services and social services for children with disabilities;
c. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for special protection of children;
d. cooperating with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of special protection of children in accordance with laws and regulations;
e. conducting rehabilitation and assistance for children who are victims of violence;
f. providing special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children victims of violence and human trafficking, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic drugs abuse, and HIV/AIDS;
g. providing inclusive education for children with special needs; and
h. taking preventive measures in order to prevent violations of children's rights.
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in the effort of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by regional apparatus that carries out authority affairs in the social field.

Article 41

1. The implementation of handling women and children victims of violence is carried out through integrated services.
2. Integrated services for women and children victims of violence use the principles of:
a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; and
l. fulfillment of women's and children's rights.

Article 42

The implementation of handling women and children victims of Violence includes services for:
a. complaint handling;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance; and/or
e. repatriation and social rehabilitation.

Article 43

1. Complaint handling services for victims of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 42 letter a shall be implemented by:
a. integrated service center for women and children empowerment;
b. hospitals;
c. community health centers;
d. children's social protection houses/shelters;
e. advisory board for marriage guidance and preservation;
f. state prosecutor's office;
g. district court;
h. child welfare institutions;
i. social welfare coordination institutions;
j. elderly welfare institutions;
k. family welfare consultation institutions; and
l. domestic violence response groups.
2. Complaints of acts of violence against women and children may be made by reporters consisting of victims, families, the community and/or community organizations in the field of protection of women and children.
3. Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may file complaints:
a. directly;
b. by telephone; and/orc. by letter.
4. In addition to handling complaints submitted by reporters, officers at service institutions as referred to in paragraph (1), are also authorized to handle victims of violence against women and children obtained through other information.

Article 49

The criteria for women and children victims of violence who require safe houses include:
a. their lives are threatened;
b. they are rejected by family or community;
c. they require intensive services but their residence is relatively far away; and/or
d. they are abandoned if not placed in a safe house.

Article 52

1. Law enforcement and legal assistance services are actions by law enforcement officers to implement laws and regulations in handling cases of violence against women and children, especially to provide sanctions against perpetrators and provide protection for witnesses and/or victims.
2. Law enforcement and legal assistance services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by prioritizing the best interests of victims.
3. Law enforcement and legal assistance services are intended to provide assistance and protection to women and children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and certainty of their legal rights.
4. Types of legal assistance services include:
a. legal assistance in the reporting process and preparation of examination reports;
b. legal assistance in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal assistance in the examination process at court;
d. legal assistance in the court hearing process until there is a final and binding decision; and
e. legal assistance in the mediation process.

CHAPTER IX

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 58

1. The community may participate in the implementation of the protection of women and children.
2. The community as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of, among others, parents, families, ninik mamak, cadiak pandai, alim ulama, bundo kanduang, customary leaders, religious leaders, mass media, and the business sector.
3. Community participation is carried out through the following means:
a. optimizing the function of community organizations in the Region;
b. conducting socialization and education on laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children;
c. providing input in the process of formulating laws and regulations in the Region;
d. conducting socialization on the prevention of child marriage;
e. conducting recovery and reintegration of women victims of violence;
f. providing information and/or reporting the occurrence of violence, discrimination, exploitation, and human rights violations against women and children to the competent authorities;
g. supervising the process of handling cases of violence, discrimination, exploitation, and human rights violations against women and children;
h. providing space for children to gather and organize to express themselves, be creative and voice their opinions;
i. supervising children in accessing various information;
j. providing products that are safe for children;
k. contributing to the fulfillment of children's rights through corporate social responsibility; and/or
l. disseminating information and educational materials that are beneficial for women and children.
